{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789837291,"nonce":"nurvsZ1rRS5g8c4ItPwrTE/dNqo=","message":"6f2185e: the subscript lever. Three things had to line up. (1) The runtime's List fast path answered count/add/[] before the site's inline cache was written, so a compile never learned the receiver was a List — every loop over a list read as cold, and no devirtualisation was possible. The fast path now records the class as kind 9. (2) count on a List inlines at the top tier to a new ListCount instruction behind the class guard, and int_loop knows its result is a u32, following it through copies and block parameters (greatest fixed point, so a size that cycles through loop parameters stays known); a counter bounded by it becomes i64. (3) The Num specialiser only counted a constant as a known Num once it had visited the block defining it, in block-index order, so i + 1 with the 1 defined in the preheader stayed a generic add and the inner counter stayed boxed; it now seeds every constant and box up front. With both counters integers, the LLVM subscript takes the i64 directly and skips the integrality test: advance() went from 9 fcvtzs to 1. NUC at load ~2.8: ash/nbody 0.94 (0.97–1.05 before), ash/mandelbrot 0.26 (0.46 — its loops went integer too), bench/nbody 0.115, delta_blue 0.076, ash/method_call 0.087. Cranelift's subscript path does not yet take the integer; that and the list type-byte/count/elements reloads (hoistable once typed) are what remain in the inner loop.","files":null}]}